quickstep NEWS - history of user-visible changes.

* Noteworthy changes in release ?.? (????-??-??) [?]

** Security review.  Changes that need an application to adapt come first
(those marked API), then those that only make the library stricter or safer.
** API: a client that checks the server's certificate against a trust store
must say whom to expect.  gq_tls_config gains no_name_check, and
gq_tls_client_new, gq_tls12_client_new and what is built on them (gq_dtls,
gq_tlsconn, gq_tlsauto, gq_conn, gq_endpoint_connect) return GQ_ERR_INVAL
for a trust store and no server_name unless it is set, or a verify_peer
callback authenticates the peer.  Before, the chain was accepted for any
name, so a certificate that any trusted CA issued for another site would do.
** API: gq_conn_stateless_reset takes the token that was matched and returns
whether it ended the connection; the tokens of connection IDs not in use, or
retired, are not looked for (RFC 9000 section 10.3.1).
** API: gq_tlsconn_eof, gq_tls12conn_eof and gq_tlsauto_eof tell the
connection that the transport reached end of file.  Without the peer's
close_notify that is a truncation (RFC 8446 section 6.1): the connection is
closed with GQ_ERR_PROTOCOL.  Call it when read returns 0.
** API: gq_endpoint_config.retry_above is by default half of max_connections
instead of off, so a busy server asks for a Retry; a value above
max_connections turns that off.  admit.retry_cid_len defaults to
conn.cid_len, and gq_endpoint_new refuses a different one.  gq_quic_admit
returns GQ_ERR_INVAL for require_retry without token keys.
** API: GQ_TP_MAX_VERSIONS is 16, and a peer's version_information list
longer than that is refused instead of cut short, which could hide a forged
Version Negotiation packet.  Configured flow control windows and stream
counts are clamped to what the protocol can carry.
** qlog: stateless reset tokens, which are secrets, are logged only when
qlog_raw is nonzero.
** libgcrypt: when gq_crypto_init does the initialising it turns off the
warning about insecure memory, so the library never writes to stderr.
Initialise libgcrypt before creating threads.
** TLS 1.3: a KeyUpdate must end its record (RFC 8446 section 5.1), as any
message that changes the keys.  A client that sent early data and is told it
was accepted refuses a suite or ALPN protocol other than the ticket's.  A
server looks at no more than 8 PSK identities of a ClientHello.  A QUIC
server refuses a ClientHello with a session ID (RFC 9001 section 8.4); a
HelloRetryRequest is checked for its legacy_version; a ChangeCipherSpec
before the first hello is refused.
** QUIC: a packet too short to be unprotected is discarded, where it closed
the connection, and the sender needed no keys to cause that.
RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID for an ID not yet sent is a PROTOCOL_VIOLATION.  A
client discards a server Initial with a token and a Retry naming the
original destination ID.  A NEW_CONNECTION_ID to a zero-length ID, and
CONNECTION_CLOSE 0x1d in an Initial or Handshake packet, are
PROTOCOL_VIOLATIONs.  Failed 0-RTT packets
count towards the AEAD integrity limit.  The first datagram a client
receives, if nothing in it is accepted, does not set the server's address.
** QUIC: a frame type in a longer encoding than it needs (a PING as 0x4001)
is refused, as RFC 9000 section 12.4 says; it was taken for the frame.
** QUIC endpoint: a connection whose first datagram held no packet that
decrypts is dropped at once rather than kept for the idle timeout, and the
timer heap no longer grows with every packet.
** QUIC streams: data lost, or acknowledged, in more separate pieces than
the bookkeeping holds is no longer forgotten (a stream could stall); some
data may be sent again instead.
** DTLS: a cleartext handshake message or ACK is ignored once protected
records can be read, a cleartext alert is believed only if it is fatal, is
not close_notify, and comes before the peer has gone past the first exchange,
and a client takes no cleartext ACK for its ClientHello.  Anyone who knew the
addresses could end a handshake, or draw a whole flight, with one small
datagram.  A ClientHello with more than 256 cipher suites no longer makes
gq_dtls_listen fail.
** API: gq_random_key fills a buffer with libgcrypt's strongest level of
randomness, as long-term keys ask for; token, ticket, cookie and reset keys
use it.
** A peer's idle timeout, in milliseconds, no longer wraps when it is made
microseconds (one value gave 384 microseconds), and a ticket from a server
whose clock is up to a minute ahead is accepted, as meant.  A DTLS 1.3 key
that has seen 2^36 forged records ends the association (RFC 9147 section
4.5.3).  The code that picks between TLS 1.3 and 1.2 no longer needs 70 KB
of stack.
** Fixes: the transcript hash of a TLS 1.3 handshake was never freed, which a
remote client could use to exhaust a server's memory; the 0-RTT replay cache
searched its whole table after enough tickets had come and gone; a
connection ID longer than 20 bytes could overflow gq_token_make; secrets in
a few places were cleared in a way the compiler may remove.
** QUIC: max_udp_payload_size is now sent as a transport parameter, with the
value of gq_conn_config.max_udp_payload.  It was never sent, so a peer assumed
65527 and its path MTU probes could outgrow a path that carries our
datagrams inside another tunnel, such as CONNECT-UDP (RFC 9298).
** gq_tlsconn_events and gq_conn_events gain an optional verify_peer
callback, as gq_dtls_events already had, so that the application can replace
the check of the server's certificate chain against the trust store on TCP
TLS (gq_tlsconn, gq_tls12conn and gq_tlsauto, for TLS 1.3 and 1.2) and on
QUIC (also through gq_endpoint_connect), to pin a certificate, to accept an
untrusted one when asked to, or in tests.  It receives the chain, its length
and the server name; 0 accepts, anything else fails the handshake with
bad_certificate (GQ_ERR_CERT).  With it set, gq_tls_config.trust may be
NULL.  Without it nothing changes.
** The peer's point on a NIST curve is checked to be on the curve, in
uncompressed form, before ECDH, in secp256r1, secp384r1 and the hybrid
groups that use them, on both the server and the client side.  libgcrypt
before 1.12.4 accepts any point (an invalid-curve attack); configure still
allows 1.12.0.
** QUIC, from the same interop failures gumdrop fixed: a server keeps its
Handshake keys until HANDSHAKE_DONE is acknowledged, and a Handshake
packet from a new address can migrate the connection.  A client Initial
probe (a PING, or a ClientHello that does not advance) is answered by
resending the oldest unacknowledged Initial and Handshake data.  0-RTT
sent before a Retry goes out again as 0-RTT.  The server's connection ID
is taken from its Initial packets, and settled by the first that carries
CRYPTO.  A server that may change version acknowledges the first Initial
in the client's version before switching.  A client moving to a preferred
address sends a PING first, moves its own traffic there, and sends its
challenge once the server has challenged.  A server challenges a new path
before answering one.  When a peer at its connection-ID limit starts using
a newer ID, the older ones are retired and a replacement is issued.  An
application close is sent only in 1-RTT once those keys exist, so the
error code is not replaced by APPLICATION_ERROR.

** Performance.  A bulk QUIC transfer between two connections in one process
runs at about 1 GB/s per core, where it ran at 70 MB/s.
** Keys keep their ciphers open.  Protecting a packet or a record opened a
libgcrypt cipher context, set the key and closed it again, for the AEAD and
for the header or record number mask, and with libgcrypt's own random
number generator every open runs an entropy poll: a QUIC packet cost 17
microseconds, ten times its encryption, and a small DTLS 1.3 record 15.
Keys now hold a context for as long as they last: the 1-RTT keys of a QUIC
connection, the keys of TLS 1.3 and 1.2 records, the epochs of DTLS 1.3 and
1.2.  The contexts are only used for the key they were made for.  API:
gq_packet_keys, gq_rec_dir, gq_rec12_dir, gq_dtls_epoch and gq_dtls12_epoch
gain a pointer to them, which makes a copy of one of those structures that has
contexts a second holder of the same count.  gq_packet_keys_move and
gq_dtls_epoch_move move one, gq_packet_keys_prepare makes the contexts of a
gq_packet_keys, and gq_packet_keys_update gives the keys it makes contexts if
the keys it starts from have them.  A record must be initialised with
gq_record_init or gq_record12_init before gq_record_set_keys, which now lets
go of the context of the keys it replaces; on memory that was never
initialised it used to work by luck, and now it does not.
** Key exchange: an ML-KEM key share, in the hybrid groups and the pure
ones, is made with coins drawn at libgcrypt's ordinary strong level, not the
strongest it asks for by default.  The strongest level gathers jitter
entropy on every call, which took 2.4 milliseconds, more than the rest of a
client handshake; the share lasts one handshake, like the elliptic curve key
beside it.  Generating X25519MLKEM768 takes 0.12 milliseconds instead of 2.5,
and a handshake 1.7 instead of 4.5.
** HMAC and HKDF are done in a single libgcrypt call that opens its context
without the entropy poll, where each opened a MAC context through libgcrypt's
MAC interface, which polls.  A handshake does dozens of them (every step of
the key schedule), and the polls were two fifths of its CPU time with
libgcrypt's own random number generator: a handshake between two
connections takes 1.3 milliseconds instead of 1.6.  Nothing is kept between
calls.
** QUIC streams: acknowledging data no longer shifts the rest of the send
buffer down, which copied a whole buffer for every acknowledged frame (256
KiB by default) and was most of the time of a transfer.  A chunk that
gq_sstream_next returns ends where the buffer wraps, and may be shorter than
asked for.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.0.3 (2026-10-18)

** gq_tls_info gains sig_scheme and client_sig_scheme: the SignatureScheme
that signed the server's CertificateVerify, and the client's, as each end
saw it (0 where there was none).  The interop programs print them, and the
interop scripts check that the scheme a scenario is about is the one that
was used.
** ML-DSA is tested against OpenSSL 3.5 and later in both directions
(integration/interop.sh) and, for DTLS 1.3, against wolfSSL built with
ML-DSA (interop-dtls.sh): ML-DSA-44, 65 and 87 certificates under a classical
CA and under an ML-DSA CA, client certificates, and private keys in each of
the forms of RFC 9881, with the signature algorithm forced on the OpenSSL
side.  Nothing needed changing in the library: the code points, the key
encodings and the empty signature context agree.
** QUIC: a handshake could stall when the packet holding the start of the
server's ServerHello was lost and the server's flight was big enough to fill
its congestion window with packets the client cannot read without it.  The
lost data was queued but never sent, because only data of the space the
timer was for could be sent past the window.  A timeout now also probes an
earlier space with data waiting.  Handshakes with the big flights of ML-DSA
(8 to 21 KB) now complete under loss, reordering and any lost datagram, in
QUIC, DTLS 1.3 and TLS over TCP, and the manual states what they cost: in
QUIC one round trip more than a classical handshake, where the server's
flight is beyond three times the client's first.
** ML-DSA (FIPS 204) signatures work whatever GnuTLS the library is built
with: they are libgcrypt's (1.12.0 and later, the build floor), where they
had needed a GnuTLS with the leancrypto backend, which few have.  The
engines now offer the ML-DSA-44, -65 and -87 signature schemes by default, and
a server can use an ML-DSA key.  The library reads what GnuTLS cannot
without the backend: the private key forms of RFC 9881 (seed, expanded key or
both, as PKCS#8 in PEM) and the key in a certificate.  A certificate chain
with an ML-DSA signature in it is still validated by GnuTLS and still needs
its backend; a classical CA over an ML-DSA key does not, but needs a GnuTLS
from 3.8.9 on, which knows the key algorithm (an older one, Ubuntu 24.04's
3.8.3, refuses it as weak: validate the chain with the verify_peer callback).
** QUIC: a packet sent in the ordinary way could be taken for a path MTU
probe, because one field of the storage it is assembled in was never cleared.
The loss of such a packet is not treated as loss of its contents (RFC 9000
section 14.4), so lost handshake data, and lost stream data, were then never
sent again, and the connection stalled: a handshake with a big flight (a
long certificate chain, an ML-DSA signature) under packet loss often never
completed.  Fixed.
** ML-DSA (FIPS 204) signatures and certificate chains, through a GnuTLS
built with the leancrypto backend, are now covered by the unit tests, and CI
runs them against such a GnuTLS (the "ML-DSA" job).  Set GQ_EXPECT_MLDSA to
make the tests fail rather than skip when the backend is missing.
** The pure ML-KEM key exchange groups of draft-ietf-tls-mlkem, MLKEM512,
MLKEM768 and MLKEM1024 (new GQ_GROUP_MLKEM512, GQ_GROUP_MLKEM768 and
GQ_GROUP_MLKEM1024), are supported in TLS 1.3, DTLS 1.3 and QUIC, for
deployments that must not rely on a classical component.  They are not in
the default list of groups: a connection uses one only when the
application puts it in gq_tls_config.groups or gq_tls_server_config.groups.
The key share is the ML-KEM encapsulation key or ciphertext alone, and a
server refuses one of the wrong length with illegal_parameter.
gq_policy_supported_groups returns every group the policy permits in its
own order (hybrids, then pure groups, then classical ones).
** Congestion control is CUBIC (RFC 9438) by default, with HyStart++ (RFC
9406) ending slow start when the round trip time starts to rise.  NewReno
remains available: set gq_conn_config.congestion_control to GQ_CC_NEWRENO.
Windows are reduced to 0.7 on loss rather than 0.5 by default, so a
connection that relied on the old behaviour will see a different one.
** Bulk stream data is paced over the round trip (RFC 9002 section 7.7)
instead of leaving as one burst of up to the whole window.  gq_conn_timeout
now also returns the time the pacer will next have credit while data is held
back, so a caller driven by it needs no change.
** The congestion window grows only while the sender is held back by the
window or the pacer, as RFC 9002 section 7.8 says, rather than by a guess
from the bytes in flight.
** gq_conn_stats gains pacing_rate_bps.
** A connection can report what it does as the events of
draft-ietf-quic-qlog-quic-events: set gq_conn_config.qlog (see
quickstep/qlog.h) and each event arrives as a connection, a time, a name
and a line of key=value fields, for awk and the other text tools.  The
detail is chosen with qlog_importance and qlog_raw; secrets are never
logged.  Every event of the draft is reported except the three that repeat
what the packet events carry.  An endpoint
reports the events that belong to no connection through the same callback.
gq_conn_original_dcid names a log by the draft's convention.
** The latency spin bit (RFC 9000 section 17.4) is implemented, so an
observer on the path can measure the round trip time: a server echoes the
bit of the newest packet it has received and a client sends its opposite.
It is off, and the bit sent at random in each packet, for one connection in
eight (the RFC requires one in sixteen) and when the connection moves to a
new path, which starts again.  Set gq_conn_config.disable_spin_bit to turn
it off for every connection.  qlog reports spin_bit_updated and the bit in
1-RTT packets.
** ECN (RFC 9000 section 13.4, RFC 9002 section 7.1) is implemented for an
application that passes on the ECN field of datagrams: gq_conn_recv_ecn and
gq_conn_send_ecn (and gq_endpoint_recv_ecn and gq_endpoint_send_ecn) take
and return it as a gq_ecn, and the existing calls are unchanged and never
mark anything.  Received codepoints are counted per packet number space and
reported in ACK frames, a packet marked CE is acknowledged at once, and 1-RTT
packets are marked ECT(0) once the path passes the validation of RFC 9000
section 13.4.2 (the first ten are the test; the handshake is never marked).
An increase in the peer's CE count reduces the window as a loss does, without
persistent congestion.  gq_conn_stats gains ecn_state, ecn_marked_sent and the
counts received, and qlog reports ecn_state_updated and the codepoint of
datagrams.  The quic-interop-runner endpoint reads and sets the ECN field of
its datagrams, and so takes part in the runner's ecn test case instead of
answering 127.
** Path MTU discovery (RFC 9000 section 14.3, RFC 8899).  Datagrams start
at 1200 bytes and, once the handshake is confirmed, probes find what the
path takes, up to gq_conn_config.max_udp_payload, which is now a ceiling and
defaults to 1452 (it was the size used, and 1200).  A caller must give
gq_conn_send a buffer of at least that for probing to take place.  The
search is made again on a change of path and after a black hole, and every
600 seconds when it ended short of the ceiling; a lost probe is not
congestion.  gq_conn_stats gains max_datagram_size and pmtu_done, and qlog
reports mtu_updated and is_mtu_probe_packet.
** Packets that arrive before the keys to read them (RFC 9001 section 5.7)
are held and read when the keys come, instead of being dropped for the peer
to send again: a Handshake or 1-RTT packet ahead of the handshake data that
brings its keys, and a 0-RTT packet ahead of the ClientHello or of the
Initial that makes the connection (the endpoint holds those for a second).
gq_conn_config.max_buffered_packets bounds it, 16 by default and negative
for none; gq_conn_stats gains packets_buffered, and qlog reports
packet_buffered and the keys_available trigger of packet_received.
** A server can offer its clients a preferred address (RFC 9000 section
9.6): gq_conn_config.preferred_address is called for each connection as it
starts, with the path of the client's first Initial, and fills in the
addresses to offer.  The connection adds a connection ID of sequence number
1, with its stateless reset token, to the transport parameters.  A client is
told by the new preferred_address event when the handshake is confirmed, and
takes the address up with gq_conn_migrate_preferred, which is allowed even if
the server set disable_active_migration.  The quic-interop-runner endpoint
offers a second port for the connectionmigration case, and any client of it
migrates to an address it is offered.
** The interop endpoint writes a log for each connection into QLOGDIR, which
the runner sets, and qlog-lint checks such files against the draft; the
interop workflow runs it on what each run wrote.
** A client can send a token of up to 512 bytes in its Initial packets.  A
token over 100 bytes (quic-go's Retry token is about 110) overflowed a
buffer in the length calculation of the packet header, so the first flight
after such a Retry could not be built and the connection failed with
"cannot build packet".
** A server that moves to a new peer address (RFC 9000 section 9.3) now
sends the PATH_CHALLENGE in the first packet to it.  The congestion window
can be full of packets lost with the old address, and only an ack-eliciting
packet carries a challenge, so an ACK-only packet used to go first and the
challenge waited for loss detection; the path switch now grants a probe
packet, which the window does not bar.
** A connection no longer ends with a spurious "stateless reset" when a
datagram that ends in zeros reaches it.  A client's first connection ID has
no stateless reset token (RFC 9000 section 18.2), but the connection kept
an all-zero token for it and compared every undecryptable datagram's last
16 bytes with it.  neqo, quic-go and ngtcp2 pad Initial datagrams with raw
zero bytes, so a retransmitted Initial reaching an established connection
killed it, and under heavy packet loss those peers' handshake loss and
corruption runs failed.
** gq_conn_config.initial_rtt_us sets the round trip time assumed before one
is measured (default 333 ms, as RFC 9002 section 6.2.2 recommends).  It sets
how soon the first probes go out and how fast they back off.  The interop
runner's endpoint uses 100 ms, and offers only X25519 in the multiconnect
case (the runner's handshake loss and corruption) so that the ClientHello
fits in one datagram: in a simulation of the runner's 30% loss in bursts of
3, the share of handshakes taking more than 5 seconds fell from 13% to 1%,
and 5 seconds is how long some servers keep a half-open handshake.
** TLS key log: gq_tls_config.keylog and gq_tls_server_config.keylog take a
callback that receives each handshake secret as a line of the NSS key log
format (CLIENT_HANDSHAKE_TRAFFIC_SECRET and the rest, for QUIC and TLS 1.3
alike) so that Wireshark can decrypt a capture.
** An endpoint for the quic-interop-runner (tests/quic-interop-runner/): a
client and server speaking hq-interop, a Dockerfile that builds it with gcc
on the runner's Ubuntu base, and a GitHub Actions workflow that runs the
runner's matrix against other QUIC implementations.  It has a loopback
smoke test in the integration suite.
** The tests are split.  make check runs only unit tests, which are
deterministic and use no real I/O or clock; the interop scripts, the
quic-interop-runner smoke test and the tests of file and system trust store
loading moved to integration/ and run with make check-integration.
** TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2 negotiate x25519 as well as secp256r1 for ECDHE
(RFC 8422): a client offers both, P-256 first, and the server picks the
first group of its own preference (the configured groups that TLS 1.2 can
do, else secp256r1 then x25519) that the client offers, writes it in
ServerKeyExchange and parses the matching ClientKeyExchange.  A client
that sends no supported_groups gets secp256r1; one with nothing in common
is refused unless it resumes (no key exchange then, and info.group is 0
for both ends).  A client refuses a ServerKeyExchange group it did not
offer, a combined TLS 1.3 / 1.2 hello included.  Policy: gq_policy_*_groups_for (1.2) is {secp256r1, x25519}.
** Ed25519 in TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2 (RFC 8422 section 5.1.3): a server whose
certificate key is Ed25519 selects an ECDHE_ECDSA suite and signs its key
exchange with it, a client verifies that, and Ed25519 client certificates
work in both directions; Ed25519 is in the 1.2 signature list and in
signature_algorithms_cert.  Interoperates with OpenSSL and GnuTLS (OpenSSL's
own DTLS 1.2 server does not serve an Ed25519 certificate).
** TLS 1.2 and DTLS 1.2 ignore warning-level alerts other than
close_notify (RFC 5246 section 7.2.2); TLS 1.3 and DTLS 1.3 ignore
user_canceled (RFC 8446 section 6) and still treat every other alert as
fatal.  GQ_ALERT_USER_CANCELED and the GQ_ALERT_LEVEL_ constants are new.
** gq_dtls_send and gq_dtls12_send send nothing for an empty write, as the
stream transports do; the receiver drops empty records anyway.
** Negotiating clients and servers: the hello kept for a fall back to
TLS 1.2 is no longer limited to 4 KB (a ticket of 2 KB and a hybrid key
share overflowed it, and the fall back then failed), and over DTLS it is
put together from its fragments.  A negotiating DTLS server without a
listener holds the first datagrams of a fragmented ClientHello until
supported_versions is in, instead of taking it for DTLS 1.2.
** Fixes found by gumdrop's interop work: a late Initial addressed to the
ID of a connection that has gone no longer makes a new server connection
(the endpoint remembers the IDs it issued for as long as the idle
timeout); a cleartext (epoch 0) DTLS handshake fragment can no longer end
a DTLS 1.2 or 1.3 association, whether it arrives after the handshake or
conflicts with another, and a cleartext ACK only acknowledges cleartext
records; DTLS 1.3 sends its alert before reporting the association
closed; GQ_SIGNATURE_MAX now holds an ML-DSA-87 signature and the client's
CertificateVerify buffer allows for it.
** QUIC conformance fixes: a client keeps its probe timeout backoff until
the server's address is validated (an Initial acknowledgement does not
count); persistent congestion is judged only on packets sent after the
first round trip sample and over several rounds of loss detection; the
peer's ACK Delay cannot wrap round into a small one; a server holds an
ack-eliciting Initial back rather than send it unpadded when the
anti-amplification credit is below a datagram, and sets no probe timer
it cannot use; peers' acknowledgements of our ACK frames retire what they
named (RFC 9000 section 13.2.4), so ACK frames stay small under loss;
when the peer retires connection IDs, no path goes on using one; the
previous key phase's read keys are dropped after three probe timeouts;
AEAD integrity and confidentiality limits close the connection
(AEAD_LIMIT_REACHED); an acknowledgement of a 0-RTT packet no longer
allows the first key update.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.0.2 (2026-09-30)

** The project is renamed from gnuquic to quickstep.  Public headers live
under include/quickstep/, the library is libquickstep, and pkg-config is
quickstep.pc.  The C API (gq_ / GQ_) is unchanged.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.0.1 (2026-09-27)

** First tarball sent for FSF evaluation (as gnuquic).

* Noteworthy changes before the first release

** Initial development.
** Version negotiation for TLS and DTLS (tlsauto.h, dtlsauto.h): by
default a server chooses between the TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2 engines (or DTLS
1.3 and 1.2) from the ClientHello, so one listener serves both and
prefers 1.3, and a client sends one hello offering both and falls back if
the server picks 1.2. The version is decided once; there is no
renegotiation into another. A 1.2 server that also serves 1.3 sets the
RFC 8446 downgrade sentinel and negotiating clients enforce it. Either end
can be pinned to one version (for a port per version, and tests). QUIC
stays TLS 1.3 only. In a combined hello the 1.2 client accepts X25519
and secp384r1 ECDHE curves as well as secp256r1 (only when it also
offered 1.3). The interop tools use the negotiating front.
** QUIC-LB connection IDs (quiclb.h, draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers-21): a
pluggable connection ID generator that encodes a routable server
identifier into every ID a connection mints (the handshake ID, each
NEW_CONNECTION_ID) and into a Retry's ID, plaintext or under AES-128 (a
single AES pass at 16 octets, a four-round Feistel construction
otherwise). Plugs into gq_conn_config.cid_gen and
gq_admit_config.cid_gen; the random default is unaffected when neither is
set. Verified against the draft's appendix B test vectors and, through a
full handshake and an active migration, that a server behind one keeps
answering to IDs that decode to its own server id throughout.
** 0-RTT (RFC 9001 section 4.6): a client that resumes a session sends
stream data and datagrams in its first flight under the remembered server
limits; a server accepts them once per ticket (single use replay cache)
and tells the application when data is early (gq_conn_early_data_active).
Rejected early data is sent again in 1-RTT packets; a server that
tightened its limits is refused. The endpoint can keep the ticket keys
and replay cache. Tested in simulation (loss, reordering, v1 and v2) and
against quiche in both directions. Also fixes the client's early
traffic secret emission in the TLS engine, which was never defined.
** DATAGRAM frames (RFC 9221) with acknowledgement and loss notification,
and the endpoint layer (endpoint.h): connections behind one socket are
routed by connection ID, new clients are admitted (Version Negotiation,
Retry always or when busy, tokens), unknown connections are answered
with rate limited stateless resets whose tokens derive from a key, and
timers are kept in a heap so idle connections cost nothing. Tested
in simulation and against quiche (four clients at once, datagrams both
ways).
** Path validation and connection migration (RFC 9000 sections 8.2 and 9):
gq_conn_recv_path and gq_conn_send_path carry opaque addresses; a server
follows a peer that moves (validating the new path, limiting what it sends
there to three times what arrived, falling back if validation fails), a
client can probe paths and migrate (gq_conn_probe_path, gq_conn_migrate),
and each path uses its own peer connection ID.  Tested in a simulated NAT
and against quiche's migration in both directions.
** QUIC version 2 (RFC 9369) is now the default preference: endpoints
list v2 then v1, a client starts in the oldest listed version and the
server follows the client's order (RFC 9368), so two endpoints of this
library talk v2. Session tickets are bound to the QUIC version (the
server derives a separate ticket key ring for v2, RFC 9369 section 5), and
the conn ticket event reports the version.
** Version negotiation: RFC 9368 version_information transport parameter,
compatible negotiation between QUIC v1 and v2 during the handshake with
downgrade protection, and the client's restart after a Version
Negotiation packet (RFC 9000 section 6.2).
** Address validation (token.h, listen.h): Retry and NEW_TOKEN tokens
bound to the client address, a stateless admission step that answers
Version Negotiation and Retry, and client handling of Retry; tested
against quiche.
** QUIC connections (conn.h) and streams (stream.h, ranges.h): the
handshake, packet number spaces, ACKs, RTT estimation, loss detection and
probe timeouts and NewReno congestion control with persistent
congestion (RFC 9002), stream and
connection flow control, connection IDs, key update, idle timeout,
connection close and stateless reset; tested against quiche in both
directions, with loss.
** DTLS 1.2 (dtls12.h, dtls12cookie.h): the TLS 1.2 profile over datagrams,
with whole-flight retransmission and a stateless HelloVerifyRequest;
tested against OpenSSL, GnuTLS and wolfSSL.
** DTLS 1.3 (dtls.h, dtlscookie.h): the TLS 1.3 engine over datagrams with
record number encryption, fragmentation, ACKs, retransmission, KeyUpdate
and a stateless address-validating cookie; tested against wolfSSL.
** TLS 1.2 (tls12.h, tls12conn.h) with the same restricted profile as
Gumdrop and Hopf: ECDHE on secp256r1 or x25519 and AEAD suites only, mandatory
extended master secret and renegotiation indication, ticket resumption.
